# Cursor Cloud specific instructions

Select these notes only for an actual Cursor Cloud task, not local Windows work.
The root `AGENTS.md` safety, data and targeted-verification rules still apply.
VM provisioning notes below are historical assumptions to verify, not installed
toolchain evidence or permission to install packages, access accounts or share.

## Toolchain

The historical Cloud image supplied Node 24 via nvm and `vp` (Vite+) with pnpm.
Verify the current VM's toolchain against `package.json` (`engines.node: ^24.13.1`)
before use. `vp` is the package/task runner; inspect the affected package scripts.

- A Cloud non-login shell may select a different Node on PATH. Inspect the actual
  executable/version; use a verified login-shell setup, not a guessed nvm path.
- Use touched-file tests/lint and affected-package typechecks. Do not run global
  `vp check --fix`, repo-wide formatting or root recursive checks by default.

## Running the web app

- Root `npm run dev` calls `scripts/dev-runner.ts dev`; `dev:server` and
  `dev:web` select one side. Read actual ports/home from `[dev-runner]` output;
  worktree offsets and occupied ports can change the selected values.
- Browser dev is single-origin through Vite. In `dev`/`dev:web`, the runner
  deletes `VITE_HTTP_URL`/`VITE_WS_URL` and sets `T3CODE_SINGLE_ORIGIN_DEV=1`.
  Desktop mode deliberately supplies loopback URLs; do not copy that setup into
  browser mode or treat it as permission to launch Desktop.
- Home precedence: explicit nonempty `--home-dir`, then worktree `.t3`, then
  ambient `T3CODE_HOME`, then the default home. Verify the resolved home is an
  isolated fixture; multiple worktrees do not necessarily share `~/.t3`.
- Pairing is required; do not describe the service as generally unauthenticated.
  Pairing URLs carry credentials. Sharing, minting tokens and private delivery
  follow root task-specific authorization, never routine setup output.
- Provider execution requires the selected external provider runtime. Inspect
  current provider configuration when relevant; this note authorizes neither
  global CLI installation nor access to real accounts/credentials.

## Android native builds (apps/mobile)

Historical Cloud provisioning (recheck before use) included OpenJDK 17
(`JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-17-openjdk-amd64`; the base image's default `java` is 21, so builds must
use `JAVA_HOME`), and the Android SDK at `$HOME/Android/Sdk` (`ANDROID_HOME`/`ANDROID_SDK_ROOT`) with
platform-tools, `platforms;android-36`, `build-tools;36.0.0`, `ndk;27.1.12297006`, and `cmake;3.22.1`.
Gradle may download pinned SDK packages. Installs, `prebuild --clean`, device
operations and EAS cloud builds require an explicitly scoped mobile task.

- Generate the native project first: from `apps/mobile`,
  `EXPO_NO_GIT_STATUS=1 APP_VARIANT=development npx expo prebuild --clean --platform android`
  (the `android/` folder is generated, not committed). RN 0.85 defaults: compileSdk/targetSdk 36,
  minSdk 24, NDK `27.1.12297006` (see `react-native/gradle/libs.versions.toml`).
- Build with Gradle from `apps/mobile/android`, e.g. `./gradlew :app:assembleDebug`. First build is
  slow (Kotlin + C++/NDK codegen). Limit ABIs to speed things up, e.g.
  `-PreactNativeArchitectures=arm64-v8a`. A historical native-module check used:
  `./gradlew :react-native-worklets:assembleDebug` compiles Kotlin + C++ and emits an AAR + `.so`s.
- Historical VM limitation: no `/dev/kvm` (recheck on the selected VM), so a hardware-accelerated Android
  emulator will not run. Use a physical device (`adb`) or EAS cloud builds (`vp run eas:android:*`) to
  actually run the app. The full IDE GUI is unnecessary here — the SDK/NDK/Gradle toolchain that
  Android Studio bundles is what is installed.
- Historical, unverified blocker for full-app `:app:assembleDebug`:
  `:react-native-screens:compileDebugKotlin` fails because `patches/react-native-screens@4.25.2.patch`
  adds new codegen props (`subtitle`, `largeSubtitle`, `navigationItemStyle`,
  `headerCenterBarButtonItems`, `headerToolbarItems`) to the JS spec + iOS native code but includes no
  Android Kotlin setters, so the generated `ScreenStackHeaderConfigManagerInterface` is unimplemented.
  The mobile README documents only iOS for local builds and routes Android through EAS. Fixing local
  Android app builds requires an app/patch change (add the Android Kotlin setters), which is out of
  scope for environment setup. The cited patch is absent in this checkout;
  do not assume this failure still exists or recreate it from these notes.

